CTX={'Dark Days': 'Two oversized one-shots that seed everything: Batman has been hiding three metals and a secret, and the Forge and the Casting are where the Dark Multiverse first presses against the door.', 'Metal': 'Barbatos drags Gotham into the Dark Multiverse and the Justice League find themselves fighting a version of Batman for every fear he ever had.', 'Gotham Resistance': 'While the League is scattered, Nightwing, the Titans, Green Arrow and the Suicide Squad fight through a Gotham remade as a nightmare funhouse.', 'The Dark Knights': 'Seven one-shots, seven ruined Earths, seven Batmen who lost. Individually they are origin stories; together they are the roster of the invasion.', 'Bats Out of Hell': 'The Dark Knights split up and take the League apart one hero at a time, running through the Flash, Hal Jordan and two issues of Justice League.', 'Lost & Found': 'Two companion one-shots working opposite ends of the same mystery: Batman lost inside his own memory, and Hawkman found at the bottom of the Dark Multiverse.', 'The Wild Hunt': "The Dark Knights hunt Detective Chimp and the last hope of the Multiverse through the Dark Multiverse itself, immediately before Metal's finale.", 'No Justice': 'The transition out of Metal. The Source Wall breaks, Brainiac splits the heroes into four colour-coded teams, and the Justice League that emerges is the one Year of the Villain will test.', 'The Totality': "Snyder's Justice League opens with a piece of the Source Wall falling to Earth and Lex Luthor deciding that humanity was built to lose.", 'Legion of Doom': "Four non-contiguous Justice League issues that hand the book to the villains. Read in sequence they are Luthor's argument with himself, and the reason he takes Perpetua's offer.", 'Good Morning, Moon': 'A single-issue breather between Totality and Drowned Earth, and a quiet reminder of what the League is actually protecting.', 'Drowned Earth': "Aquaman, the Titans and the League against alien sea gods who drown the world. It matters here because it is where the League's losing streak begins.", 'The Batman Who Laughs': 'The breakout villain of Metal gets his own miniseries, plus the Grim Knight one-shot. This is where the infection plot that runs into Death Metal is actually built.', 'Escape From Hawkworld': 'Hawkgirl, Martian Manhunter and the Justice League go into the Source Wall itself. The Annual sits mid-arc rather than after it.', 'The Sixth Dimension': 'Superman, Jarro and a version of the League that already won. The arc that sets up why Perpetua can be freed at all.', 'Year of the Villain Special': 'A twenty-five-cent one-shot in which Lex Luthor makes his offer to the entire DC Universe. Short, and the hinge the whole bridge turns on.', 'Apex Predator': 'Luthor completes his transformation and Perpetua begins to move. Three issues, and the last quiet moment before the Justice/Doom War.', 'Justice/Doom War': 'Eleven issues of Justice League running end to end: the League against the Legion of Doom across time, ending with the loss that leaves the door open for Death Metal.', 'Who Are the Secret Six?': 'Batman and Superman hunt the six heroes the Batman Who Laughs has infected. The connective spine between Year of the Villain and Death Metal.', 'The Infected': 'Four one-shots following individual corrupted heroes. Read alongside Batman/Superman rather than after it.', 'The Infected: Supergirl': "Kara's infection plays out in her own title, with Wonder Woman trying to talk her down and the story deliberately finishing somewhere else.", 'The Infected: Hawkman': 'Carter Hall loses himself to Sky Tyrant, a past life the infection brings to the surface. His own book carries the whole arc.', 'Hell Arisen': 'The Batman Who Laughs and Apex Lex finally collide. Four issues that end Year of the Villain and hand directly to Death Metal.', 'Year of the Villain one-shots': "Eight character-branded one-shots taking Luthor's offer to individual villains. Uneven, largely optional, but Black Adam ties into the Infected plot.", 'City of Bane': "Tom King's Batman finale. Bane holds Gotham with Flashpoint Thomas Wayne at his side. Concurrent with the bridge rather than part of it.", 'Death and the Speed Force': "The new Forces destabilise the Speed Force and the Black Flash comes collecting. Sets up the Rogues' upgrade.", "Rogues' Reign": "Captain Cold takes Central City and remakes it. The Flash's own Year of the Villain payoff.", 'The Flash: Year One (coda)': "The closing issue of Williamson's retold origin, sitting just before the bridge proper begins.", 'Wonder Woman (pre-Loveless)': 'Two Wonder Woman issues that precede Loveless. DC Database scopes Loveless to #77-81, so these sit outside it.', 'Loveless': "Cheetah kills Aphrodite with the God Killer and love itself goes out of the world. Diana's Year of the Villain arc.", 'The Wild Hunt (WW)': 'A single issue opening the arc that follows Loveless, included because it falls inside the bridge window.', 'Amnesty': 'Aquaman gives sanctuary to forgotten sea gods while Mera holds Atlantis together.', 'Manta vs. Machine': "Black Manta returns with new hardware. Two issues closing out Aquaman's part of the window.", 'Deathstroke R.I.P.': "Priest's Deathstroke run reaches its end, with Slade's family arguing over what he was.", 'The Trials of Harley Quinn': 'Harley against a tribunal of her own history, ending in the Villain of the Year one-shot.', 'Lesser Gods': "Black Lightning, Katana and the Outsiders against Ra's al Ghul, with Batman watching from a distance.", 'Oracle Rising': 'Barbara Gordon starts rebuilding the Oracle identity while Gotham burns around her.', 'Greetings from Gotham': "Tomasi's Detective Comics run in anthology mode, a string of standalone Batman cases carrying the banner.", 'Cold Dark World': 'Mister Freeze and Nora take the centre of Detective Comics for five issues.', 'The Witching War': 'Justice League Dark against the Otherkind, with Wonder Woman holding a magical front the main League cannot reach.', 'Lords of Order (coda)': 'The closing issue of the arc before the Witching War, inside the bridge window by date.', 'Generation Outlaw': 'Jason Todd mentoring a new set of Outlaws and finding out what he actually believes.', 'Prince of Gotham (coda)': 'The final issue of the preceding Red Hood arc, caught by the bridge window.', 'The Tomorrow War': 'The Terrifics against Doc Dread, with Tom Strong along for the ride.', 'The God Game (coda)': 'The last issue of the Terrifics arc that precedes the Tomorrow War.', 'Leviathan Hunt / Metropolis Doom': "Action Comics runs the Leviathan investigation alongside Luthor's offer. Adjacent to the bridge rather than inside its plot.", 'Seek and Destroy': "Robin's black-site prison catches up with the Teen Titans and splits the team.", 'Justice League Odyssey': 'Cyborg, Starfire, Azrael and Jessica Cruz in the Ghost Sector, running parallel to everything else.', 'The Gray Son Legacy': 'Dick Grayson with his memory gone, working as Ric while the Court of Owls builds replacement Talons.', 'Catwoman': 'Selina in Villa Hermosa, running her own book at the edge of the event.', 'Darkness Within': "Venditti's Hawkman before the infection takes hold, chasing his own past lives.", 'Supergirl (pre-Infected)': "Kara's issues before the Batman Who Laughs reaches her, including her part in Event Leviathan.", 'The House of El (coda)': 'A single Superman issue inside the window, from the Unity Saga rather than the bridge plot.', 'Death Metal': 'Perpetua has won, the Earth is a Batman-shaped ruin, and Wonder Woman goes looking for a way to undo every Crisis at once.', 'The Darkest Knight': "Five one-shots widening the world of Death Metal: the Guidebook, Speed Metal, Trinity Crisis, Multiverse's End and Legends of the Dark Knights.", 'Doom Metal': 'Five Justice League issues running alongside the core series, following the heroes left behind in the wasteland.', 'The Multiverse Who Laughs': 'The Batman Who Laughs completes his ascension. Robin King, Rise of the New God and the Secret Origin sit here.', 'War of the Multiverses': 'The last two one-shots before the finale: fifty-two war stories and the last stories of the DC Universe.', 'Generations': 'Two prestige one-shots bridging Death Metal to what comes after, with heroes pulled out of their own eras.', 'Infinite Frontier': 'The Crises are undone and the multiverse is remade whole — the direct aftermath of how Death Metal ends.', 'Dark Nights: Metal': 'Barbatos and the Dark Multiverse break into the waking world, and seven nightmare Batmen come with them.', 'The Bridge (Year of the Villain)': "Lex Luthor takes Perpetua's offer and the Batman Who Laughs works the shadows, while every corner of the DC Universe feels the pressure. Note the Barebones path skips this era entirely.", 'Dark Nights: Death Metal': 'The Earth is a scorched Batman-shaped ruin, Perpetua rules, and the last heroes fight for the right to exist.'}
